ms of \\( u \\):\n\n\\[ u^2 - 5u + 6 = 0. \\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( u = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -5 \\), and \\( c = 6 \\):\n\n\\[ u = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{25 - 24}}{2} = \\frac{5 \\pm 1}{2}. \\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions for \\( u \\):\n\n\\[ u = \\frac{5 + 1}{2} = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad u = \\frac{5 - 1}{2} = 2. \\]\n\nRecall that \\( u = x^2 \\), so we have:\n\n\\[ x^2 = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x^2 = 2. \\]\n\nTaking the square roots of both sides, we get the \\( x \\)-coordinates of the intersection points:\n\n\\[ x = \\pm \\sqrt{3} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\pm \\sqrt{2}. \\]\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that the \\( x \\)-coordinates of the intersection points are \\( \\pm \\sqrt{m} \\) and \\( \\pm \\sqrt{n} \\) where \\( m > n \\). Therefore, we can identify \\( m = 3 \\) and \\( n = 2 \\).\n\nThe difference \\( m - n \\) is:\n\n\\[ m - n = 3 - 2 = 1. \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{1}. \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:263"}
